## Problem
Building failures in NYC are rarely one party's mistake. They happen when architects, engineers, geotechs, contractors, and DOB each miss something, because critical information about a site, its neighbors, and similar past failures is scattered across slow, siloed systems. Teams don't know what to watch for until it's too late.
 
## Solution
A shared, browser-based risk and coordination tool that:
1. Pulls DOB history for a site and its adjacent buildings
2. Matches the project against a library of past NYC failures
3. Generates a project-specific checklist of coordination items, each assigned to a discipline
4. Delivers every risk through a roast character, with a serious explanation underneath
## MVP Scope
 
### In
- [ ] Address lookup returning site and adjacent buildings' violations, complaints, and stop work orders
- [ ] Project inputs: type (new build, renovation, excavation adjacent to existing), building age, foundation type if known
- [ ] Curated precedent library of 10 to 20 NYC failures, tagged by building type, conditions, and root cause
- [ ] Risk checklist from matching inputs plus site data against precedents, each item assigned to a discipline
- [ ] Map view of site, neighbors, and nearby precedent incidents
- [ ] Roast layer with serious explanation under every item
### Out (Roadmap)
- Plan examiner objection prediction
- Scanned historic plan analysis
- Early structural schemes for massing options
- DOB-side portal
